625, 0.051422119140625, -0.0184783935546875, 0.00986480712890625, 0.007030487060546875, 0.024078369140625, -0.0272674560546875, -0.002658843994140625, 0.0246124267578125, -0.00965118408203125, 0.0161895751953125, -0.0175933837890625, 0.03790283203125, 0.03839111328125, 0.026092529296875, 0.007419586181640625, 0.01309967041015625, -0.00641632080078125, 0.0235595703125, 0.04473876953125, -0.04693603515625, -0.0023651123046875, -0.0421142578125, -0.05120849609375, 0.0102996826171875, -0.007320404052734375, 0.005535125732421875, -0.006839752197265625, 0.01129150390625, -0.0023193359375, 0.015625, -0.0140533447265625, -0.019683837890625, -0.01349639892578125, 0.01027679443359375, 0.0004215240478515625, 0.022369384765625, -0.01305389404296875, 0.003936767578125, -0.002231597900390625, 0.0144805908203125, -0.00717926025390625, 0.00539398193359375, 0.0361328125, -0.00563812255859375, 0.016510009765625, -0.0177459716796875, 0.0009899139404296875, 0.021392822265625, 0.00011330842971801758, -0.0025768280029296875, 0.005207061767578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, there is a research gap regarding the effectiveness and interpretability of in-house small language models (SLMs) compared to larger, external models. The main objective of this study is to evaluate whether in-house SLMs can provide accurate, secure, and interpretable invoice categorisation, and to understand the relationship between embedding geometry and classification performance in this domain.\n\n**Background and Definitions:**  \nRecent advances in natural language processing have enabled the use of sentence transformers and SLMs for document classification. In this context, \"anisotropic\" refers to an embedding space where data points are unevenly distributed, while \"isotropic clusters\" are locally dense, uniform groupings within that space. \"Structured input\" denotes invoice data reformatted with explicit fields and labels, as opposed to unstructured, free-text input.\n\n**Methodology:**  \nWe analysed a proprietary financial corpus of 12,000 invoices from multiple clients, each labelled with GL codes and vendor identities. Preprocessing included anonymisation, tokenisation, and removal of non-informative fields. We evaluated two pre-trained models: a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa). Both models were fine-tuned on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU for 10 epochs, using a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of 2e-5. Evaluation was performed using 5-fold cross-validation, with accuracy, F1 score, and 95% confidence intervals reported. Baselines included a zero-shot large language model (LLM; GPT-3.5, prompted without fine-tuning) and a vendor identity baseline (assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor).\n\n**Results:**  \nGeometric analysis revealed that the sentence-embedding space is globally anisotropic but contains locally isotropic clusters, with clusters strongly correlated to vendor identity. SBERT fine-tuned on our dataset achieved an average accuracy of 0.96 (\u00b10.01) and F1 score of 0.94 (\u00b10.02), outperforming the zero-shot LLM (accuracy 0.85, F1 0.81) and the vendor identity baseline (accuracy 0.88, F1 0.83). Notably, SBERT improved classification for smaller, challenging categories and for new clients, reaching an F1 score of 0.90 (\u00b10.03) with approximately 100 client-specific invoices. The structured input format, which included explicit field labels and tabular formatting, did not improve SLM performance compared to unstructured text; this counterintuitive result suggests that the model\u2019s pre-training favours natural language patterns over artificial structure.\n\n**Discussion and Limitations:**  \nOur findings indicate that in-house SLMs can achieve high accuracy and generalisation in invoice categorisation, with embedding geometry linked to classification performance. However, limitations include the proprietary nature of the dataset, potential overfitting to specific vendors, and the lack of external validation. The structured input result may be model-specific and warrants further investigation. Future work should explore generalisability to other financial domains, robustness to adversarial inputs, and the impact of larger or more diverse datasets.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nIn-house SLM implementations, such as fine-tuned SBERT, show promise for secure and interpretable invoice categorisation, outperforming both zero-shot LLMs and simple vendor-based heuristics. Nonetheless, further research is needed to confirm these findings across broader settings and to address the observed limitations."}}